= Voltage — Technical =

== Potential difference ==
In electrostatics, electric potential V is the electric potential energy per unit test charge, up to an arbitrary additive reference constant. Between points A and B:

<pre>
V(B) - V(A) = [U(B) - U(A)] / q
</pre>

The SI unit is J/C. Potential differences are independent of the chosen zero of potential in the electrostatic model.

== Circuit approximation ==
An ideal voltmeter has very high input resistance, limiting loading of the measured circuit. A source's open-circuit voltage need not equal its terminal voltage under load because of internal impedance and other dynamic effects. In a passive-sign convention, P = V I indicates power absorbed by the component; other reference choices reverse the sign.

== Time-varying fields ==
For a conservative electrostatic electric field, voltage between two points is path-independent. In time-varying magnetic fields, induced electric fields can have nonzero circulation and a single globally path-independent electrostatic potential difference is insufficient. Such contexts require a specified path or more complete electromagnetic description.

